Subject: [PATCH] fritchii2-lastaddr: a core's write area in HandleRasterizerWrite (last_address) now holds for one

fritchii2-lastaddr: a core's write area in HandleRasterizerWrite (last_address) now holds for one
gather only, so guest CPU writes to a page that no longer holds GPU-written data are marked
CPU-modified at once, instead of waiting for the next gather while the tracker holds the page
clean. Switch
EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR=1, off by default.

Upstream: the diagnosis is Frizti/Fritchii 9e8cb73ede (Frizti, 2026-11-07, "buffer_cache:
EDEN_NX2_EXP9, check native uniform bank pages only when they may have changed", in fritchii-v4):
HandleRasterizerWrite keeps each core's last_address on a page once a write to it was collected
and never resets it, so later writes to that page are only collected, not marked CPU-modified,
until the next InvalidateGPUCache. Fritchii never changed last_address (still the same at
fritchii-v5.2, 18a68f26ad); it worked around it in its bank check (EXP9 flags, our
fritchii2-bankflag) and in v5 with EXP35 (collect all writes, apply at each FlushCaching). So this
reset is our own fix of the mechanism Fritchii found; there is no upstream line to cherry-pick.
It is the narrow core of our EDEN_UP_HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD (MythosOfs/Orchid f2eb04babd, whose
CollectWrite reports a page again after each gather) without that switch's download hold-back.
t71-o ran HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD only without the frame wait (dark flashes, no effect).

Mechanism. OnCPUWrite answers true for a page the buffer cache holds GPU-modified: the write is
collected and applied at the next gather (GPU::InvalidateGPUCache: fences, MemOpB, guest reads of
GPU data). The core then sets last_address to that page and skips OnCPUWrite for every later
write there. When the page later loses its GPU-written data (download, gather), those writes are
still only collected: the tracker holds the page clean, so a draw binding it before the next
gather uploads nothing and reads the older host bytes. The back is often caught up with the game
(GPU back ~37% waiting for the front, front ~23% waiting for the game, more so with the full frame
wait), so a gather often runs before the game writes the words of its next list. Those writes then
miss every draw of that list.

With =1:
- GPUDirtyMemoryManager::Gather moves Generation on (as it does for HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD).
- HandleRasterizerWrite (the default path, not the HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD one) keeps the generation with
  last_address. A write to the same page in a later generation asks OnCPUWrite again. If the page
  is still GPU-modified, the write is collected as before and the area is armed for this
  generation. If not, OnCPUWrite marks the write CPU-modified (buffer, texture and pipeline caches)
  and does not collect it, as for a write to any other clean cached page.
- Log, at each power of two: "EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR: N writes to a core's last collected page
  asked again after a gather, M of them taken at once". M counts the writes the old path held back
  while the page was clean. If M stays 0 for a session, the mechanism does not fire in our build.

Unset: Generation() is not read (seen = 0) and every area's generation stays 0, so the test is the
old `last_address != subaddress`. Gather leaves the counter alone and the HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD path is
untouched, so behaviour is byte-identical. The checks run T82's text and the patched text through
the same model (random writes, GPU writes, downloads and gathers) and get identical traces.

Cost on the PS5 (guest cores are the limit, 82-88% busy). Per core and gather, at most one extra
OnCPUWrite (the buffer cache mutex) for a page that is still GPU-modified. A page found clean costs
one OnCPUWrite (both cache mutexes). After that the page is CPU-modified and unprotected, so the
following writes skip HandleRasterizerWrite until the next upload protects it again. The old path
sent each of those writes through the slow path to Collect instead. Expect about no fps change;
measure the walk fps and read the log counts.

Bright or dark: this leaves the host copy too OLD, never too new. A draw gets the bank words from
before the game's latest write: the previous user of that slot (another object's material, or
last frame's). That is the white-material flash kraibse saw in Ocarina of Time (Eglantine
031e07436c, about 1 in 500 presents) and Fritchii v4's "fewer white flashes". It lasts one frame
at most: the next gather (several a frame) or the next frame's once-a-frame bank re-mark
(EDEN_NX2_BANK_REUPLOAD unset = 1) applies the write. Our dark full-scene flashes were the
opposite, too new (frame N drawn with N+1's words), and the full frame wait removed them. The
wait makes the back catch up with the game every frame, which widens this too-old window. So rare
bright one-frame flashes with the wait on fit this mechanism. That is plausible, not proven: the
log shows whether it fires. Not covered: writes to pages that are still GPU-modified wait for the
gather by design (EDEN_NX2_HELD_WRITES=1, a gather before each list, covers those too).
One trade (review 9 Nov): a write found clean is no longer collected, so if the back uploads the
page in the short gap between OnCPUWrite's mark and the guest's store, nothing applies that write
later (until the page's next write or the once-a-frame bank re-mark). Every first write to any
clean page already has this gap; the old collect closed it for these writes only, at the cost of
holding them until the gather.
Independent of fritchii2-bankflag; the two apply on T82 in either order.

diff --git a/src/core/gpu_dirty_memory_manager.h b/src/core/gpu_dirty_memory_manager.h
--- a/src/core/gpu_dirty_memory_manager.h
+++ b/src/core/gpu_dirty_memory_manager.h
@@ -76,10 +76,21 @@
     /// EDEN_UP_HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD: moves on with each Gather. A page the core reported to the GPU
     /// before must be reported again on its next write: the write held back for it has been
     /// applied. Read after Collect, it tells whether the write went into the list after a Gather.
+    /// EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR moves it on too (ResetWriteAreas); with neither it stays 0.
     [[nodiscard]] u64 Generation() const {
         return generation.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
     }
 
+    /// EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR=1 (diagnosis: Frizti/Fritchii 9e8cb73ede): Memory's
+    /// HandleRasterizerWrite asks OnCPUWrite again on a core's first write to its last collected
+    /// page after each Gather. Off: as before, every later write to that page is only collected
+    /// (applied at the next Gather) until the core collects a write to another page, even once
+    /// the page holds no GPU-written data any more.
+    [[nodiscard]] static bool ResetWriteAreas() {
+        static const bool on = Common::PeSwitch("EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR");
+        return on;
+    }
+
     /// The page a core last reported to the GPU, and in which generation.
     struct ReportedPage {
         PAddr page{~PAddr{}};
@@ -134,7 +145,7 @@
         {
             std::scoped_lock lk(guard);
             TransformAddress t;
-            if (HeldVsDownload()) {
+            if (HeldVsDownload() || ResetWriteAreas()) {
                 // Before the exchange: a Collect that lands in the next list sees the new
                 // generation.
                 generation.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
@@ -225,7 +236,7 @@
     }
 
     std::atomic<TransformAddress> current{};
-    std::atomic<u64> generation{}; ///< EDEN_UP_HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD only
+    std::atomic<u64> generation{}; ///< EDEN_UP_HELD_VS_DOWNLOAD / EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR only
     std::mutex guard;
     std::mutex gather_guard;
     std::vector<TransformAddress> back_buffer;
diff --git a/src/core/memory.cpp b/src/core/memory.cpp
--- a/src/core/memory.cpp
+++ b/src/core/memory.cpp
@@ -924,29 +924,63 @@
             });
             return;
         }
+        // EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR (diagnosis: Frizti/Fritchii 9e8cb73ede): last_address spares
+        // OnCPUWrite for the page whose writes the GPU collects, but it stays on that page after a
+        // Gather applied them and the page lost its GPU-written data. Later writes there are then
+        // still only collected, so the buffer cache holds the page clean and draws with its older
+        // bytes until the next Gather. With the switch the area holds for one Gather only.
+        const bool reset_areas = Core::GPUDirtyMemoryManager::ResetWriteAreas();
         gpu_device_memory->ApplyOpOnPointer(p, scratch_buffers[core], [&](DAddr address) {
             auto& current_area = rasterizer_write_areas[core];
             PAddr subaddress = address >> YUZU_PAGEBITS;
+            // Unset: 0 here and in every area, so only the page decides, as before.
+            const u64 seen = reset_areas ? gpu_dirty_managers[core].Generation() : 0;
+            const bool same_page = current_area.last_address == subaddress;
             // Performance note:
             // It may not be a good idea to assume accesses are within the same subaddress (i.e same page)
             // It is often the case the games like to access wildly different addresses. Hence why I propose
             // we should let the compiler just do it's thing...
-            if (current_area.last_address != subaddress) {
+            if (!same_page || current_area.generation != seen) {
                 // Short circuit the need to check for address/size
                 auto const do_collection = (address != 0 && size != 0)
                     && system.GPU().OnCPUWrite(address, size);
+                if (same_page) {
+                    NoteWriteAreaReset(!do_collection);
+                }
                 if (do_collection) {
                     current_area.last_address = subaddress;
+                    current_area.generation = seen;
                 } else {
                     return;
                 }
             }
             gpu_dirty_managers[core].Collect(address, size);
         });
+    }
+
+    /// EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR: counts the writes to a core's last collected page asked again
+    /// after a Gather, and those OnCPUWrite took at once (no GPU-written data left there: marked
+    /// CPU-modified now, where the old path only collected them for the next Gather), logged at
+    /// each power of two.
+    static void NoteWriteAreaReset(bool taken_at_once) {
+        static std::atomic<u64> asked{};
+        static std::atomic<u64> at_once{};
+        const u64 at_once_count =
+            at_once.fetch_add(taken_at_once ? 1 : 0, std::memory_order_relaxed) +
+            (taken_at_once ? 1 : 0);
+        const u64 count = asked.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed) + 1;
+        if ((count & (count - 1)) == 0) {
+            LOG_INFO(HW_Memory,
+                     "EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR: {} writes to a core's last collected page asked "
+                     "again after a gather, {} of them taken at once (the page had no GPU-written "
+                     "data left; the old path held them for the next gather)",
+                     count, at_once_count);
+        }
     }
 
     struct GPUDirtyState {
         PAddr last_address;
+        u64 generation; ///< EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_LASTADDR: Generation() when last_address was set
     };
 
     void InvalidateGPUMemory(u8* p, size_t size) {
